Encoder**: Google Vertex AI Text Embedding Model (768-dim)\n- **Function**: Extract semantically rich event descriptions based on location/time\n- **Strategy**: Frozen pretrained encoder preserves generalization knowledge\n- **Output**: Event text embeddings (E_text)\n\n### Module 3: Fusion & Alignment\n- **Mechanism**: Cross-attention with residual connections\n- **Purpose**: Deeply integrate traffic and event representations\n- **Key Innovation**: Traffic (Query) attends to Events (Key/Value)\n- **Output**: Fused embeddings (H_fused)\n- **Unified Dimension**: 1024 (for both modalities)\n\n### Module 4: Decoder\n- **Architecture**: Simple fully-connected layer\n- **Input**: Fused embeddings\n- **Output**: Traffic forecasts for future timesteps\n\n---\n\n## 3. Repository Structure\n\n### Minimal Research Release\nThe repository contains only example baseline configurations:\n\n```\nrepos/FUSE-Traffic/\n├── README.md                          # Minimal (1 line)\n├── gemini.py                          # Event processing with Gemini API\n└── baselines/\n    └── D2STGNN/\n        ├── METR-LA.py                 # Config for 207 sensors\n        ├── PEMS-BAY.py                # Config for 325 sensors\n        └── PEMS03.py                  # Config for 358 sensors\n```\n\n**Key Files**:\n\n| File | Location | Size | Purpose |\n|------|----------|------|---------|\n| gemini.py | `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/gemini.py` | 150 lines | Event extraction via Google Gemini API |\n| METR-LA.py | `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/baselines/D2STGNN/METR-LA.py` | 158 lines | Configuration template for METR-LA |\n| PEMS-BAY.py | `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/baselines/D2STGNN/PEMS-BAY.py` | 158 lines | Configuration template for PEMS-BAY |\n| PEMS03.py | `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/baselines/D2STGNN/PEMS03.py` | 158 lines | Configuration template for PEMS03 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technical Specifications\n\n### Framework & Dependencies\n\n**Core Framework**: PyTorch (via basicts library)\n\n**Key Libraries**:\n- `basicts`: Custom traffic forecasting framework\n  - D2STGNN model implementation\n  - TimeSeriesForecastingDataset\n  - Metric functions (masked_mae, masked_mape, masked_rmse)\n- `google.genai`: Google Generative AI SDK (Gemini API)\n- `vertexai`: Vertex AI embeddings\n- `pandas`: Data manipulation and HDF5 I/O\n- `numpy`: Numerical operations\n- `easydict`: Configuration management\n- `tqdm`: Progress tracking\n\n**External Services**:\n- Google Cloud Platform (GCP)\n- Gemini 2.5 Pro API (event extraction)\n- Vertex AI Text Embedding Model (text-embedding-005)\n\n### Data Requirements\n\n**Traffic Data Format**: HDF5 (.h5)\n- Structure: N×T tensor (sensors × timesteps)\n- Temporal resolution: 5-minute intervals\n- Preprocessing: Z-score normalization\n\n**Graph Data Format**: Pickle (.pkl)\n- Content: Adjacency matrices (double-transition)\n- Type: Weighted spatial dependency matrices\n\n**Event Data Format**: Compressed Numpy (.npz)\n- Embeddings: 768-dimensional float32 vectors\n- Generated via: Google Vertex AI API\n\n**Sensor Locations Format**: CSV\n- Columns: index, sensor_id, latitude, longitude\n- Usage: Geographic context for event queries\n\n### Datasets Supported\n\n| Dataset | Sensors | Timesteps | Data Type | Time Span |\n|---------|---------|-----------|-----------|-----------|\n| METR-LA | 207 | 34,272 | Traffic Speed | Mar-Jun 2012 |\n| PEMS-BAY | 325 | 52,116 | Traffic Speed | Jan-Jun 2017 |\n| PEMS03 | 358 | 26,208 | Traffic Flow | Sep-Nov 2018 |\n\n### Model Hyperparameters\n\n**Architecture**:\n- Input sequence length: 12 steps (60 minutes)\n- Output sequence length: 12 steps (60 minutes)\n- Hidden dimension: 32\n- Dropout: 0.1\n- Number of ST layers: 5\n- Temporal kernel: 3, Spatial kernel: 2\n- Temporal embedding: 10-12 dimensions\n- Event embedding: 768 dimensions (Vertex AI)\n- Unified fused embedding: 1024 dimensions\n- Attention heads: 8 (inferred)\n\n**Training Configuration**:\n- Optimizer: Adam (lr=0.002, weight_decay=1e-5)\n- Loss: Masked MAE\n- Scheduler: MultiStepLR (milestones: [1,30,38,46,54,62,70,80], gamma=0.5)\n- Epochs: 100\n- Batch size: 64-128 (dataset-dependent)\n- Gradient clipping: max_norm=5.0\n- Early stopping: patience=10 epochs\n\n**Curriculum Learning**:\n- METR-LA: 0 warm + 6 CL epochs\n- PEMS-BAY: 30 warm + 3 CL epochs\n- PEMS03: Standard curriculum applied\n\n---\n\n## 5. Performance Results\n\n### Benchmark Comparison (METR-LA Dataset)\n\n| Rank | Model | 15 min MAE | 30 min MAE | 60 min MAE | Avg MAE | Type |\n|------|-------|-----------|-----------|-----------|----------|------|\n| 1 | **FUSE-Traffic** | **2.53** | **2.90** | **3.39** | **2.94** | GNN+LLM |\n| 2 | T3 | 2.56 | 2.98 | 3.46 | 3.00 | GNN+Text |\n| 3 | D2STGNN | 2.57 | 2.99 | 3.42 | 3.00 | GNN only |\n| 4 | STORM | 2.64 | 3.00 | 3.45 | 3.03 | GNN+Text |\n| 5 | GraphWaveNet | 2.70 | 3.09 | 3.52 | 3.10 | GNN only |\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**Overall Performance**:\n- 2.0% average improvement over best GNN baseline (D2STGNN)\n- 3.0% average improvement over best fusion baseline (STORM)\n- Consistent across all prediction horizons\n\n**Robustness on Event-Driven Scenarios**:\n- Performs best on high-impact events (5-10% of timestamps)\n- Shows 4.2-15% improvement over baselines during disruptions\n- Captures rapid traffic changes (accidents, weather)\n\n**Ablation Studies Results**:\n\n1. **Prompt Design**:\n   - Full feature (P1): Best performance\n   - Weather exclusion: 5-10% MAE increase\n   - Crime exclusion: 3-5% MAE increase\n\n2. **Fusion Mechanism**:\n   - Cross-attention: Best (reference)\n   - Gating: 3-4% worse\n   - Simple addition: 2-3% worse\n   - Concatenation: 5-8% worse\n\n3. **Hyperparameters**:\n   - Optimal MLP layers: 2\n   - Optimal embedding dimension: 512\n\n---\n\n## 6. Event-Aware Processing Pipeline\n\n### Event Retrieval Strategy\n\n**Prompt Template**:\n```\nYou are a traffic analysis assistant for [CITY].\nYou need to consider events occurring Hin to (Hout+Hin-1) minutes \nafter each timestamp that could affect traffic.\n\n### Date and Time: [TIMESTAMP]\n### Geographic Coordinates: [SENSOR_COORDS]\n\nFor each sensor, identify nearby events that could impact traffic \n(e.g., LA news, Severe Weather, concerts, crime).\n```\n\n**Default Configuration**:\n- Future event window: 60-115 minutes\n- Event types: News, weather, crime, concerts/exhibitions\n- Output: JSON with event descriptions per sensor\n\n### Event Impact Classification\n\n**Categories**:\n- No Impact: ~60% of timestamps\n- Minor Impact: ~10-15%\n- Moderate Impact: ~15%\n- High Impact: ~5-10%\n\n**LLM-Extracted Features**:\n- Event category (location inference)\n- Event time window\n- Weather conditions\n- Event names and details\n- Impact estimation\n\n---\n\n## 7. Integration Pathway for LibCity\n\n### Recommended Migration Steps\n\n**Step 1: Adapt D2STGNN**\n- Import D2STGNN implementation\n- Adapt to LibCity's model interface\n- Maintain multi-graph support\n- Preserve 5-layer ST decomposition\n\n**Step 2: Implement Fusion Module**\n- Create CrossAttentionFusion class\n- Multi-head attention with 8 heads\n- Layer normalization and feed-forward\n- Unified 1024-dim embedding space\n\n**Step 3: Replace Google APIs**\n- Option A: Local LLM (Ollama, LLaMA)\n- Option B: Local embeddings (sentence-transformers)\n- Option C: Cache precomputed event embeddings\n\n**Step 4: Configuration Migration**\n- Convert EasyDict to YAML/JSON\n- Support dataset-specific parameters\n- Enable curriculum learning options\n- Maintain hyperparameter sets\n\n**Step 5: Data Integration**\n- Ensure HDF5 compatibility\n- Load adjacency matrices from pickles\n- Integrate event embeddings\n- Handle sensor coordinates\n\n### Key Challenges\n\n1. **External Dependency**: Google Cloud APIs\n   - Solution: Use local open-source alternatives\n   \n2. **basicts Library**: Not part of LibCity\n   - Solution: Extract and adapt D2STGNN implementation\n   \n3. **Event Data Generation**: Expensive at scale\n   - Solution: Pre-compute and cache event embeddings\n   \n4. **Model Complexity**: 4 modules + attention\n   - Solution: Modular architecture with pluggable components\n\n---\n\n## 8. Comparison with Related Approaches\n\n### Event-Aware Traffic Forecasting Methods\n\n| Method | Modalities | Event Data | Fusion | Alignment |\n|--------|-----------|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| **FUSE-Traffic** | ST + Text | LLM (dynamic) | Intermediate | Cross-Attention |\n| STORM | ST + Text | Manual (static) | Intermediate | Gating |\n| T3 | ST + Text | Manual (static) | Output | None |\n| UrbanGPT | ST + Text | LLM-based | Input (prompt) | None |\n| DIGC-Net | ST only | Manual incidents | Intermediate | None |\n| D2STGNN | ST only | - | - | - |\n| GraphWaveNet | ST only | - | - | - |\n\n### Architectural Innovations\n\n1. **Dynamic Event Querying**: Only FUSE-Traffic queries LLM per timestamp\n2. **Efficient Fusion**: Cross-attention is more efficient than end-to-end LLM\n3. **Frozen Text Encoder**: Preserves generalization from pretraining\n4. **Future-Looking Events**: 60-115 min ahead (anticipatory)\n\n---\n\n## 9. Key Insights for Implementation\n\n### What Makes FUSE-Traffic Effective\n\n1. **Separation of Concerns**:\n   - GNN handles structured traffic patterns\n   - LLM handles unstructured event understanding\n   - Attention handles fine-grained fusion\n\n2. **Prompt Engineering**:\n   - Explicit event types guide LLM\n   - Examples improve reasoning\n   - Geographic + temporal context essential\n\n3. **Frozen Encoder Strategy**:\n   - Reduces trainable parameters\n   - Prevents overfitting on sparse event data\n   - Maintains broad semantic understanding\n\n4. **Curriculum Learning**:\n   - Helps on difficult datasets (PEMS-BAY)\n   - Gradual complexity increase\n   - Improves long-horizon predictions\n\n### Critical Design Decisions\n\n1. **Why Cross-Attention?**\n   - Allows traffic data to query event information\n   - More selective than concatenation\n   - More efficient than LLM-only approaches\n\n2. **Why Freeze Text Encoder?**\n   - Text data is sparse relative to traffic data\n   - Freezing prevents overfitting\n   - Preserves broad semantic knowledge\n\n3. **Why Future Events (60-115 min)?**\n   - Anticipatory forecasting needs\n   - Non-causal knowledge integration\n   - Practical for real-time systems\n\n4. **Why Multi-Modal Embedding?**\n   - Enables deep interaction between modalities\n   - Better alignment than separate representations\n   - Improves interpretability\n\n---\n\n## 10. Research Contribution Summary\n\n### Novel Aspects\n\n1. **First LLM + GNN fusion** for event-aware traffic forecasting\n2. **Dynamic event querying** per spatio-temporal context\n3. **Cross-attention alignment** for effective multimodal fusion\n4. **Comprehensive ablation studies** on prompt design and mechanisms\n\n### Practical Impact\n\n- Demonstrates that LLM world knowledge improves traffic predictions\n- Shows event understanding is essential for robustness\n- Proves cross-attention superior to simpler fusion methods\n- Validates curriculum learning for difficult datasets\n\n### Generalization\n\n- Tested on 3 major traffic datasets (METR-LA, PEMS-BAY, PEMS03)\n- Consistent improvements across prediction horizons\n- Superior performance especially on high-impact events\n- Robust to different event types and frequencies\n\n---\n\n## 11. Files and Resources\n\n### Analyzed Files\n1. **gemini.py** (150 lines)\n   - Location: `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/gemini.py`\n   - Event extraction via Google Gemini API\n   \n2. **METR-LA.py** (158 lines)\n   - Location: `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentCity/repos/FUSE-Traffic/baselines/D2STGNN/METR-LA.py`\n   - Configuration template for METR-LA\n   \n3. **PEMS-BAY.py** (158 lines)\n   - Location: `/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/agentC